/* ============================================================================
   hypergeometric-pmf.epher -- sampling without replacement, exactly

   Draw n objects without replacement from a population of N that holds
   K special objects. The number X of special objects in the sample is
   hypergeometric: choose k of the K special ones and n - k of the N - K
   others, out of all ncr(N, n) samples, so

     P(X = k) = ncr(K, k) * ncr(N - K, n - k) / ncr(N, n).

   There is no hypergeometric builtin in epher, so the formula is the
   method. The urn example (10 balls, 4 red, draw 4) keeps every answer
   an exact fraction; the deck example is the classic aces problem (52
   cards, 4 aces, 5-card hand) and prints as decimals because its exact
   denominators exceed 1000.

   Source: Feller, "An Introduction to Probability Theory and Its
   Applications", volume 1, chapter 2.

   Run it from a terminal:
     Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/probability/distributions/hypergeometric-pmf.epher
     Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\probability\distributions\hypergeometric-pmf.epher"
     macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/probability/distributions/hypergeometric-pmf.epher
   Web/desktop:             paste the file into the entry, press Enter
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- helpers ----
def hg(k, N, K, n) = ncr(K, k) * ncr(N - K, n - k) / ncr(N, n)
// P(exactly k special objects in n draws without replacement from N,
// of which K are special); all arguments are exact integers.

// ---- urn: 10 balls, 4 red, draw 4 without replacement ----
// The five point probabilities, as exact fractions that sum to 1.
for k in 0 to 4 do hg(k, 10, 4, 4)

// The hand-checkable case: P(2 red) = 6 * 15 / 210 = 3/7.
print("P(exactly 2 red):", hg(2, 10, 4, 4))

// ---- deck: aces in a five-card hand (52 cards, 4 aces) ----
// P(exactly 2 aces) = ncr(4, 2) * ncr(48, 3) / ncr(52, 5), about 0.0399;
// the exact fraction is 103776/2598960 = 2162/54145.
print("P(exactly 2 aces in 5 cards):", hg(2, 52, 4, 5))
print("P(no aces in 5 cards):", hg(0, 52, 4, 5))

// At least one ace: the complement of the "no aces" line above.
print("P(at least one ace):", 1 - hg(0, 52, 4, 5))

// The probability of at least one ace is 1 - ncr(48, 5)/ncr(52, 5) =
// 1 - 1712304/2598960 = 0.341158001662, about one hand in three.
/* ---- expected output ----
= {1/14, 8/21, 3/7, 4/35, 1/210}
= P(exactly 2 red): 3/7
= P(exactly 2 aces in 5 cards): 0.0399298180811
= P(no aces in 5 cards): 0.658841998338
= P(at least one ace): 0.341158001662
*/
